# =====================================================================
#  Monoram Jewellers — Apache settings for cPanel shared hosting
#
#  This file is not a program. It is a short list of instructions that
#  the web server reads every time somebody opens the site.
#
#  It must sit in the SAME folder as index.html on the server — usually
#  public_html, but see DEPLOY.md step 6 if the domain points somewhere
#  else. If this file is missing, the site still works, but a phone can
#  keep showing an old copy of the design for days.
#
#  Every block below is wrapped in <IfModule>. Shared hosts differ in
#  which Apache modules they switch on, and an unguarded directive for a
#  module that is off makes the whole site return "500 Internal Server
#  Error". Guarded like this, a missing module means that one block is
#  skipped and everything else still works.
# =====================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#  1. File types
#
#  Some servers do not recognise these and send them as plain text,
#  which makes the browser refuse to load them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
  AddType application/manifest+json  .webmanifest
  AddType text/javascript            .js
  AddType text/javascript            .mjs
  AddType image/svg+xml              .svg
</IfModule>


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#  2. What the phone may keep, and for how long
#
#  This is the block that matters most. A whole round of finished work
#  once reached the shop looking unstyled because the phone had kept an
#  old styles.css: the new index.html and app.js arrived, the stylesheet
#  did not, and the new parts of the page rendered with no design at all.
#
#  HOW IT WORKS NOW. Every deploy works out one short code from the
#  contents of the site — something like a3f19c4d — and writes it into
#  index.html, so the page asks the server for
#
#      styles.css?v=a3f19c4d      app.js?v=a3f19c4d
#
#  instead of plain styles.css. Change one letter of one file and the
#  code changes, every address on the page changes with it, and a phone
#  that has never seen those addresses cannot possibly have an old copy
#  of them. Change nothing and the code stays the same, so a phone that
#  has been here before downloads nothing at all.
#
#  That is why the two rules below look contradictory and are not:
#
#    * A file ASKED FOR WITH a ?v= code may be kept for a year, because
#      that exact address will never hold different contents.
#    * The very same file asked for WITHOUT one must be re-checked every
#      time, because then there is nothing in the address to tell an old
#      copy from a new one.
#
#  index.html itself never carries a code — it is the address the
#  customer types. It is the one file that must always be re-checked,
#  and it is the file that names the version of everything else. That is
#  what makes it impossible to end up running a new app.js against an
#  old lib.js: they are named together, by one file, in one breath.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Is there a ?v=... on the end of this request? If so, remember it as
# VERSIONED, which the two rules below then ask about.
#
# This has to be mod_rewrite. The obvious alternative, SetEnvIf, cannot
# do it: SetEnvIf reads request headers and a short list of special
# values, and the query string is in neither — its Request_URI is the
# path only, with everything after the ? already stripped. Written that
# way it silently matches nothing, every file quietly falls back to
# being re-checked, and the fix looks like it works while doing nothing.
# It was written that way first, and only testing against a real Apache
# caught it.
#
# No address is rewritten here. The "-" means "change nothing"; the rule
# exists only for the [E=...] flag on the end, which sets the marker.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On
  RewriteCond %{QUERY_STRING} (^|&)v=[A-Za-z0-9._-]+
  RewriteRule \.(js|css)$ - [E=VERSIONED:1]
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_headers.c>

  # --- Fingerprinted scripts and the stylesheet. ---
  # env=VERSIONED  -> asked for as app.js?v=a3f19c4d : keep for a year.
  # env=!VERSIONED -> asked for as plain app.js      : re-check always.
  #
  # If mod_rewrite is switched off, VERSIONED is never set, the second
  # line applies to everything, and the site falls back to re-checking
  # every file on every visit. Slower, still correct, never stale.
  #
  # config.js is deliberately NOT in this list. It carries the Supabase
  # address and key, it is the one file that might ever be corrected by
  # hand in the cPanel File Manager, and it is under a kilobyte. It gets
  # a ?v= code like the rest — so a deploy always lands — but it is also
  # re-checked every visit, so an edit made on the server lands too.
  <FilesMatch "^(styles\.css|app\.js|lib\.js|viewer\.js|theme\.js|marks\.js|ambient\.js|admin\.js)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable" env=VERSIONED
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate" env=!VERSIONED
    # If mod_expires is also on, it will have added an Expires date in the
    # past for these. Cache-Control outranks Expires in every browser, but
    # a header saying one thing and another saying the opposite is asking
    # for trouble on a host we cannot inspect. Drop it.
    Header unset Expires env=VERSIONED
  </FilesMatch>

  # --- The pages and the connection settings: always re-checked. ---
  # "no-cache" does NOT mean "do not keep it". The phone still stores the
  # file and still shows it instantly — it just asks the server "has this
  # changed?" first. When it has not, the reply is an empty 304 and
  # nothing is downloaded again.
  <FilesMatch "^(index\.html|admin\.html|preview\.html|config\.js|manifest\.webmanifest)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>

  # --- Pictures, icons and fonts: kept for a year. ---
  # These are safe to keep, because a changed picture gets a new address
  # from the database rather than replacing an old one at the same name.
  # Keeping them is what makes the site open quickly on mobile data.
  <FilesMatch "\.(png|jpe?g|gif|webp|avif|ico|svg|woff2?|ttf|otf|eot)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
  </FilesMatch>

  # --- The service worker: never kept, not even for a second. ---
  # LAST ON PURPOSE. This one file decides which version of everything
  # else the phone is allowed to use offline, and a cached service worker
  # cannot be replaced by a new one — it keeps running last month's rules
  # about the whole site. sw.js is a .js file, so it would otherwise be
  # caught by a rule above; coming last, this block overwrites whatever
  # they set. "no-store" tells the browser to keep no copy whatsoever.
  <FilesMatch "^sw\.js$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate, max-age=0"
    Header set Pragma "no-cache"
    Header set Expires "0"
  </FilesMatch>

</IfModule>

# The same instruction again, for hosts that have mod_expires switched on
# but not mod_headers. Deliberately conservative: it tells every script and
# stylesheet to be re-checked, ignoring the ?v= code, because mod_expires
# cannot see a query string. On such a host the site is a little slower and
# completely correct — which is the right way round to fail.
#
# When both modules are on, mod_headers runs later and its Cache-Control
# replaces the one set here, so the year-long rule above still applies.
<IfModule mod_expires.c>
  ExpiresActive On
  ExpiresByType text/html                   "access plus 0 seconds"
  ExpiresByType text/css                    "access plus 0 seconds"
  ExpiresByType text/javascript             "access plus 0 seconds"
  ExpiresByType application/javascript      "access plus 0 seconds"
  ExpiresByType application/manifest+json   "access plus 0 seconds"
  ExpiresByType image/png                   "access plus 1 year"
  ExpiresByType image/jpeg                  "access plus 1 year"
  ExpiresByType image/gif                   "access plus 1 year"
  ExpiresByType image/webp                  "access plus 1 year"
  ExpiresByType image/avif                  "access plus 1 year"
  ExpiresByType image/svg+xml               "access plus 1 year"
  ExpiresByType image/x-icon                "access plus 1 year"
  ExpiresByType font/woff2                  "access plus 1 year"
  ExpiresByType font/woff                   "access plus 1 year"
</IfModule>


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#  3. Keep the working files private
#
#  The .sql files describe the database and the .md files are your own
#  notes. Neither belongs on the public internet. deploy.sh and
#  deploy.ps1 already refuse to upload them; this is a second lock in
#  case one is ever put there by hand.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
  <FilesMatch "\.(sql|md|sh|ps1|example)$">
    Require all denied
  </FilesMatch>
  <FilesMatch "^\.env">
    Require all denied
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>
<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
  <IfModule mod_access_compat.c>
    <FilesMatch "\.(sql|md|sh|ps1|example)$">
      Order allow,deny
      Deny from all
    </FilesMatch>
    <FilesMatch "^\.env">
      Order allow,deny
      Deny from all
    </FilesMatch>
  </IfModule>
</IfModule>


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#  4. Do not list the contents of a folder
#
#  Without this, opening a folder that has no index.html shows a listing
#  of every file in it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_autoindex.c>
  Options -Indexes
</IfModule>


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#  5. If the phone STILL shows an old page
#
#  Everything above is what this site can control. A shared host can add
#  a cache of its own in front of it, and then its answer wins over ours.
#  This cannot be checked from here — it has to be looked at on the live
#  server. Three things to look for, in order:
#
#  1. cPanel -> "Optimize Website" / "LiteSpeed Cache" / "LScache".
#     Many cPanel accounts run LiteSpeed rather than Apache. LiteSpeed
#     reads this file and obeys mod_headers, but its own page cache sits
#     in FRONT and can serve a saved copy of index.html without asking.
#     If there is a LiteSpeed Cache panel, either turn it off for this
#     account or add index.html to its "do not cache" list.
#
#  2. Cloudflare, or any other proxy in front of the domain. In the
#     Cloudflare dashboard the domain shows an orange cloud when its
#     cache is in the path. Caching -> Configuration -> Purge Everything
#     once, then set Browser Cache TTL to "Respect Existing Headers".
#     Leaving it on "4 hours" overrides everything in this file.
#
#  3. What the server actually replies. On a computer, open the site,
#     press F12, the Network tab, tick "Disable cache" off, reload, and
#     click index.html. The Response Headers must show
#         cache-control: no-cache, must-revalidate
#     and app.js?v=... must show
#         cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#     If either shows something else — a max-age on index.html, or an
#     "x-litespeed-cache: hit" line — the answer is coming from a cache
#     in front of Apache and points 1 and 2 are where to look.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------


# =====================================================================
#  DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: the https:// redirect, and the www redirect.
#
#  An earlier version of this file forced https itself. That has been
#  removed on purpose. cPanel hosts almost always redirect to https
#  already, either in AutoSSL or in the account's own settings, and two
#  redirects pointing at each other put the browser in a loop and the
#  site stops loading entirely — "too many redirects".
#
#  If the site is genuinely reachable over plain http with no redirect,
#  turn it on in cPanel (Domains → the domain → Force HTTPS Redirect)
#  rather than adding it back here. Doing it in one place cannot loop.
# =====================================================================
